      "text": "To determine the period of the function \\( y = \\tan x + \\cot x \\), we need to find the value of \\( T \\) such that \\( y(x + T) = y(x) \\) for all \\( x \\).\n\nFirst, recall the definitions and properties of the tangent and cotangent functions:\n- The function \\( \\tan x \\) has a period of \\( \\pi \\), meaning \\( \\tan(x + \\pi) = \\tan x \\).\n- The function \\( \\cot x \\) also has a period of \\( \\pi \\), meaning \\( \\cot(x + \\pi) = \\cot x \\).\n\nNow, let's consider the function \\( y = \\tan x + \\cot x \\). We need to find the smallest positive value \\( T \\) such that:\n\\[ y(x + T) = y(x) \\]\nfor all \\( x \\).\n\nSubstitute \\( x + T \\) into the function:\n\\[ y(x + T) = \\tan(x + T) + \\cot(x + T) \\]\nWe need:\n\\[ \\tan(x + T) + \\cot(x + T) = \\tan x + \\cot x \\]\n\nTo satisfy this equation, we need to consider the periodicity of the tangent and cotangent functions. Specifically, we need to find \\( T \\) such that:\n\\[ \\tan(x + T) = \\tan x + \\text{some term involving } T \\]\nand\n\\[ \\cot(x + T) = \\cot x + \\text{some term involving } T \\]\n\nUsing the periodicity properties:\n\\[ \\tan(x + T) = \\tan x + \\text{some term involving } T \\]\nand\n\\[ \\cot(x + T) = \\cot x + \\text{some term involving } T \\]\n\nGiven that the tangent and cotangent functions have a period of \\( \\pi \\), we need to find the smallest \\( T \\) such that both terms involving \\( T \\) cancel out or combine to give zero. This requires:\n\\[ T = \\pi \\]\n\nTo confirm, let's check if \\( T = \\pi \\) works:\n\\[ y(x + \\pi) = \\tan(x + \\pi) + \\cot(x + \\pi) \\]\nSince \\( \\tan(x + \\pi) = \\tan x \\) and \\( \\cot(x + \\pi) = \\cot x \\), we have:\n\\[ y(x + \\pi) = \\tan x + \\cot x = y(x) \\]\n\nThus, the period of \\( y = \\tan x + \\cot x \\) is \\( \\pi \\).\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\pi}\n\\]",
